    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently overseeing the acquisition of a new collection of rare 19th-century manuscripts for our university archive here in Belgium. I'm trying to decide whether we should implement the Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC) or move toward the Library of Congress Classification (LCC) for this specific collection. Could you provide a technical comparison of the two systems, specifically regarding their scalability for specialized research collections and their ability to handle granular metadata for historical manuscripts?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "DDC is a numerical system offering broad organization, which can sometimes lack the necessary specificity for highly detailed historical manuscripts. Conversely, LCC is an alphabetic system that provides more granular subject and format distinction, making it superior for specialized, fine-grained cataloging needs. For rare, nuanced collections, LCC's structure typically accommodates more complex subject headings and hierarchical metadata better than DDC. Therefore, LCC is often preferred in academic settings dealing with intensive historical research materials.",
      "source": "target",
