    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that overview. One thing that concerns me is the potential for \"chaos\" during the initial transition. If we have a large volume of previously uncatalogued, miscellaneous manuscripts that don't fit neatly into existing categories, how do these two systems handle that kind of initial disorder or lack of structure? I'm worried about items getting \"lost\" in the system because they don't have a clear home.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Both systems require judicious cataloging practices to manage initial disorder, but LCC often proves more flexible for outliers. In DDC, misplaced items might fall into general, overly broad numerical ranges, leading to ambiguity. LCC's structure allows for the application of detailed \"marginalia\" or specific classification notes for items that defy neat categorization. Essentially, LCC permits a more nuanced descriptive field to keep unique or unclassified manuscripts positioned logically without forcing them into a rigid, poorly fitting slot.",
      "source": "target",
